  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef and two of his friends are going on a biking trip this new year. They decided to rent three bikes from a shop.\n\nThe shop has NNN bikes, the iii-th of which has a mileage of MiM_iMi​.\n\nChef and friends decided that they would rent three distinct bikes i,j,ki, j, ki,j,k satisfying the following property:\n(Mi⊕Mj)&amp;Mk=Mi⊕(Mj&amp;Mk)(M_i \\oplus M_j) \\mathbin{\\&amp;} M_k = M_i \\oplus (M_j \\mathbin{\\&amp;} M_k)(Mi​⊕Mj​)&amp;Mk​=Mi​⊕(Mj​&amp;Mk​)\n\nwhere ⊕\\oplus⊕ denotes bitwise XOR and &amp;\\mathbin{\\&amp;}&amp; denotes bitwise AND.\n\nPlease help them find out the number of different ways in which they can select the bikes.\n\nNote that two ways are considered to be different if the ordered triples of indices (i,j,k)(i, j, k)(i,j,k) are distinct, so for example (1,2,3)(1, 2, 3)(1,2,3), (1,2,4)(1, 2, 4)(1,2,4) and (1,3,2)(1, 3, 2)(1,3,2) are all distinct even if the bikes are such that M1=M2=M3=M4=1M_1 = M_2 = M_3 = M_4 = 1M1​=M2​=M3​=M4​=1.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line contains a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nEach test case consists of two lines:\nThe first line contains an integer NNN, denoting the number of bikes available.\nThe next line contains NNN space-separated integers M1,M2,…,MNM_1, M_2, \\ldots, M_NM1​,M2​,…,MN​ denoting the mileage of each bike.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output a single line containing one integer - the number of ways to choose a triple of bikes.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤51 \\leq T \\leq 51≤T≤5",
      "3≤N≤1053 \\leq N \\leq 10^53≤N≤105",
      "0≤Mi≤1060 \\leq M_i \\leq 10^60≤Mi​≤106",
      "Sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 10510^5105"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3\n4 5 6\n3\n1 2 4\n6\n2 6 8 9 10 13",
        "output": "2\n0\n24",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 111: The two ways to select three bikes are (1,2,3)(1,2,3)(1,2,3) and (1,3,2)(1,3,2)(1,3,2) (note that the triples are of indices, not values).\nTest Case 222: There are no ways to select three bikes satisfying the given condition.\nTest Case 333: Some ways to select three bikes are:\n\n(1,2,5)(1, 2, 5)(1,2,5)\n(1,5,2)(1, 5, 2)(1,5,2)\n(3,1,6)(3, 1, 6)(3,1,6)\n(4,3,6)(4, 3, 6)(4,3,6)\n(3,2,4)(3, 2, 4)(3,2,4)\n(1,6,5)(1, 6, 5)(1,6,5)"
      }
    ]
